lots into  [Music]  into different categories based on the  kinds of data that  they handle this is important because  in order to unify geographics and data  visualization functions  [Music]  two of the tools that we have are the  data specification for these plots and  the different  options that control the different  layers of the plots so we'll be taking a  look at both of them  but let's start with classifying the  different  data specifications so if we wanted to  do a geoplot  the data can be categorized into four  broad  classes the first one is locations this  is  virtually identical to the geographics  example that i showed you so we can  specify the locations either as a  geoposition  or an entity or a graphics primitive  like polygon  or a geoprimitive like geodesk  and we can use all of them pretty much  directly in geographics  but we have some functions that  use that in geo visualization as well so  we'll take a look at that  now if we wanted to add more information  to this basic  location information the way to do that  is to  one way to do that is to associate  each of those positions with a scalar  value and so that  brings us to a second class of  data um  that will be handled by geoviz functions  and that is scalar values and locations  so here the locations again are  specified as geoposition and entity in  polygon  but each of those positions are then  associated with a  scalar value or a color for certain  plots or quantity  and the last line  in that specifies  you know how we can do the association  of a list of positions with a list of  values  for convenience  we can of course also associate the  positions  with not just a scale value but a vector  value and so that brings us to the third  class  of data that we have and so we can have  geovectors which in themselves have  a position in the vector associated with  those positions  so we have geovector geovector e and u  or we can have a list of vectors or an  association of location with vector  and there are a class of plots that deal  with these  and then finally we have location  intensity of points which is a different  way of  visualizing locations and so  these are kind of identical to  what we have for the locations except  for  the last line there which i'll come to  in a minute but  these will pass through some  visualization functions  which will give us information about the  density  of the locations and so forth the last  one  that we have is an association where we  associate each of these locations with  the weight and so we can visualize the  location intensities based on the  weights  okay so those are the the four broad  categorizations  of uh data for the different plots so  now let's look at the different plots  and how each of these plots  handle these data types  so the first one is locations so this is  very similar to  geographics and so here we have  um the same kind of locations from  geoposition  and entity that are being passed into  geolist plot  and gls plot is going to take  the geoposition in and plot it as a  point  and so is um and so is the entity being  plotted as  a point we can also feed them  geodisc or disk and they will  plot those primitives um overlaid on top  of the  geographics so here  this is pretty much identical to what  geographics does but here you can  already see  a small difference in that we are able  to use  geo labels as an option and geolabels  is able to figure out in the second case  where we have  entity as one of the ways to specify the  location  it has enough that that entity has  enough information for gls plot to  figure out that it's able to do  something intelligent  with geo labels and it's able to name  label the point the location of that  city with the name of the city  this of course can be done in  geographics too using geostyling etc  but from the point of view of  data visualization it's more convenient  and more natural to be able to use  options that we're used to with  