anations there  and finally here is the specification  that  associate weights with the locations so  that we can get the  intensity of the locations and so here  we have  a histogram where the locations of the  capital cities  are weighted by their city populations  and so  we get a  [Music]  histogram a geohistogram where the bins  are located  at the position at the locations  and we have the bins that tell us how  many  how much population falls into each of  these bins  here we have geo smooth histogram which  takes the data and i just have the data  in a different format here but  slightly different data for capital  cities of the eurozone countries  and their corresponding populations  and so that's what the data looks like  and if we fit it  into geosmooth histogram then we get the  kernel  kernel density of the locations  weighted by their populations and then  we can overlay that on top of  the geographics  so the last class of data that we  looked at was vector values at locations  and so um let's begin by constructing  a geo vector and  we just pick 100 random locations  uh in the world and we associate  each of those locations with a vector  value  we can of course plot this directly  using geographics in the geomarker  and each of those vectors are plotted as  an arrow and  using geographics and we can set the  geoprotection  etc but if we wanted to  discern some pattern um in the in the  vectors what we  use is a geovector plot which then takes  the  array of those vectors and plots the  vector field and so here we have  the direction of the arrows showing the  direction of the  vector field and the color of the arrows  represents the magnitude of the vector  field at that position  and this is uh something that we changed  recently  for a vector plot where which moved away  from  using the size of the arrows to  represent the magnitude of the vector  field to using color  and so that's something that we can  directly inherit into a geo vector plot  a